                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_root_right:    `<strong>That's the right diagnosis.</strong> The staff don't have an evidence gap, they have an adoption gap — workload, weak local ownership, and a message that ignored the night shift. Now you can fix what's actually broken instead of repeating the 2024 training that died at 28%.`,
                    coach_r1_training:      `<strong>You've commissioned another teaching session.</strong> But your own sepsis team already published this — the staff don't have an evidence gap, they have an adoption gap. The 2024 attempt also ran training and died at 28%. What about this unit's context stops people using what they already know?`,
                    coach_r1_individuals:   `<strong>Careful — blaming individuals is the comfortable answer.</strong> When 72% of a 240-strong workforce isn't using a known bundle, that's a system and context problem, not careless people. Diagnose the workload, ownership and message, not the staff.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named it as an adoption problem AND located the highest-leverage barrier in context — culture, leadership and workload differ on every unit. That is exactly the lens that lets you design a plan that lands differently in three different places.`,
                    coach_r1_evidence:      `<strong>The evidence is settled.</strong> The bundle is a well-established national standard your own team helped prove. Re-litigating it wastes your 90 days. The barrier is context and facilitation, not the science.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>That's how you get the same 28%.</strong> 'Launch it harder' is precisely what failed in 2024. There absolutely is a barrier — and it lives in the context of each unit, not in repetition.`,
                    coach_r1_lowhonesty:    `<strong>This is how greenwashing of change starts.</strong> Low willingness to name the adoption problem means you'll run training and call it a plan. Name the real blockers honestly or you'll repeat the lapse.`,
                    coach_r2_localdata:     `<strong>Local data feedback is powerful.</strong> Showing each unit its own missed-bundle cases and times makes the gap undeniable and owned locally — far stronger than a generic instruction from management.`,
                    coach_r2_narrative:     `<strong>The clinical narrative can move people</strong> — used carefully and ethically, the coroner case gives the change human weight. Pair it with local data so it's emotion plus evidence, not emotion alone.`,
                    coach_r2_mandate:       `<strong>Mandate-and-audit buys you a number, not belief.</strong> Order it and audit hard and you'll see compliance rise on paper while staff chart the bundle without owning it — and it collapses the week the auditors leave.`,
                    coach_r2_email:         `<strong>An email and a poster is not a change plan.</strong> That's roughly what 2024 amounted to. Translation means embedding the bundle in the workflow and the people, not announcing it.`,
                    coach_r2_expertonly:    `<strong>You've put the professor in front of the night shift.</strong> They nod, then go back to doing it their way — he's never worked a 3am resus with two nurses down. Who on that shift do their peers actually trust, and what changes if that person carries the bundle?`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>The peer carries it, the expert and sponsor back it.</strong> A trusted night-shift charge nurse moves practice where the consultant can't, with the expert lending authority and the manager clearing the path. That is deliberate opinion-leadership.`,
                    coach_r2_manageronly:   `<strong>A sponsor who clears the path is useful — but they don't carry the bundle.</strong> Leaning mostly on managerial authority reads as top-down and risks the same distrust. Put a credible peer at the front.`,
                    coach_r2_study:         `<strong>You're re-proving settled evidence again.</strong> Commissioning a fresh local review repeats the 2024 instinct and burns budget on the one thing nobody disputes. Spend on embedding it in the workflow instead.`,
                    coach_r3_pilot:         `<strong>Pilot-and-spread respects the active role of context.</strong> Starting on keen Ward A lets you learn and adapt before tackling the hard ED night shift — so the rollout improves rather than failing identically everywhere at once.`,
                    coach_r3_bigbang:       `<strong>An identical big-bang ignores context.</strong> Ward A has a stable team and a keen sister; the ED night shift is short-staffed and burned out. Why would the same intervention land the same way in three different contexts? You'll get compliance on Ward A and resistance in the ED.`,
                    coach_r3_edfirst:       `<strong>Leading with your hardest unit and no learning is brave but costly.</strong> The burned-out ED night shift is where you most need an adapted, peer-led approach — going there cold risks an early, visible failure that poisons the spread.`,
                    coach_r3_contextfit:    `<strong>This is context-sensitive design.</strong> Piloting first AND flexing the how while protecting the clinical what means the ED can fit the bundle to triage flow without losing the steps that save lives. That's the fidelity-adaptation balance done right.`,
                    coach_r3_rigid:         `<strong>Total fidelity drives workarounds.</strong> Forbid any change and the ED says it doesn't fit their triage flow, so they work around it — and you're back to paper compliance. Flex the <em>how</em> without losing the clinical <em>what</em>.`,
                    coach_r3_anything:      `<strong>Adaptation without limits loses the intervention.</strong> If every unit rewrites the bundle freely, you no longer have the bundle — the clinical core that actually saves lives gets diluted away. Protect the what.`,
                    coach_r3_nofeedback:    `<strong>No feedback loop means you fly blind.</strong> Without weekly unit-level data and course-correction you won't see the change slipping until day 90 — far too late to fix it. Feedback is the engine of sustainable adoption.`,
                    coach_r3_noprotect:     `<strong>You've mandated change with no protected time.</strong> Telling a burned-out night shift to add steps with no backfill produces resentment and paper compliance, not adoption. Protect the frontline time you're asking them to spend.`,
                    coach_r4_leadlag:       `<strong>Leading plus lagging indicators is the right measurement spine.</strong> Real-time bundle steps tell you if it's slipping before the outcome data confirms it — and time-to-antibiotics and outcomes prove it's real, not paper.`,
                    coach_r4_lagonly:       `<strong>Lagging-only measurement is too slow.</strong> Monthly compliance % satisfies the regulator but won't warn you when adoption decays — and it's the easiest number to game into paper compliance.`,
                    coach_r4_launch:        `<strong>A launch dashboard then moving on is the classic decay pattern.</strong> The last initiative died the moment attention moved. Measurement has to outlive the launch or the bundle slides back to baseline.`,
                    coach_r4_champion:      `<strong>Relying on a champion you can't keep isn't sustainment.</strong> The 0.6 WTE ends at day 90. If the bundle depends on one central person, it decays when they go. Embed ownership where the work happens.`,
                    coach_r4_nobody:        `<strong>No named owner is how initiatives decay.</strong> 'Momentum will carry it' is exactly what was said in 2024. Without a named owner per ward and a place in induction and handover, this slides back the moment you look away.`,
                    coach_r4_honest:        `<strong>That's a board-ready, honest answer.</strong> You showed real belief rising with leading-indicator evidence, named owners per ward, and a plan for the night shift — that's a trajectory that survives, not a number that collapses.`,
                    coach_r4_coerce:        `<strong>You've ordered compliance and you're auditing hard.</strong> The numbers look good for the regulator, but staff are charting the bundle without believing in it. Is that real adoption that survives, or a paper trajectory that collapses the week the auditors leave?`,
                    coach_r4_declare:       `<strong>Compliance hit 70% in week two — a great launch.</strong> But your 0.6 WTE ends at day 90 and the last initiative decayed the moment attention moved on. Who owns this on each ward in month six, and how do you even see if it's slipping?`,

